{"cases":[{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"52b9711b5d73e8e20a91651210495786a9a73d02f6f5465506540684aced0efd"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 proposes stripping UNDECIDED to a derived state, but the skeptical fixed-point semantics (spec §4) is three-valued: the fixed-point operator is defined over IN/OUT/UNDECIDED jointly, and determinism of the fixed point depends on the monotone iteration that converges precisely because UNDECIDED is a first-class lattice value absorbing unresolved conflicts. Collapsing the semantics to IN/OUT-only with UNDECIDED derived does not obviously preserve the fixed point's determinism: without UNDECIDED as a lattice element, the stripped two-valued operator has no canonical absorbing value for cyclic attack structures, so a node attacked by its own attacker (odd-length cycle) no longer has a unique limiting assignment — the iteration can oscillate or require an external tie-break that reintroduces exactly the algorithmic artifact SRC_002 sought to eliminate. Thus the claim 'keeps all four guarantees' is unsupported for at least the determinism guarantee.","target":"ba25ad6fbd89196163e692d595941b3175aad80443d0fa2a57abb7e07a5ea4ac"}]}